Switch: throttle the boot pipeline warm-up behind the loading screen (aurora_switch_set_warmup_throttle)

After the loading screen (the priority set) the warm-up builds the remaining ~900 known pipelines
back to back until the first PLAY scene. Each build holds the single GL context for 0.1-0.3 s (Mesa
20.1 compiles and links from source; nouveau has no threaded compile, so
GL_KHR_parallel_shader_compile cannot help, and a second, shared context is unsafe on this
nouveau), and the render worker needs that context for every frame: the logos and title ran at
4-7 frames/s for about three minutes (hardware log gpu-default-handheld).

aurora_switch_set_warmup_throttle(duty) holds back warm-up builds (those no draw is waiting for):
after each one the compile thread stays idle for (1 - duty) / duty times its duration, then starts
the next right after a present (or 100 ms later without one), so a build overlaps the game
thread's next frame instead of landing in the middle of one. A pipeline a draw asks for is never
held back (it ends the wait). aurora_switch_get_precompile reports the duty, the builds held back
and the time waited. Off (0) unless the harness sets it.

Switch only (switch/native/aurora/patches); not for upstream.

